'''''Parietaria officinalis''''', the '''eastern pellitory-of-the-wall''',<ref name=BSBI07>{{BSBI 2007 |access-date=2014-10-17}}</ref> also known as '''upright pellitory'''<ref>{{PLANTS|id=PAOF|taxon=Parietaria officinalis|accessdate=30 January 2016}}</ref> and '''lichwort''', is a plant of the [[Urticaceae|nettle]] family. Its leaves, however, are non-stinging. The plant grows on rubbish and on walls, hence the name. 

The pollen is a cause of [[allergy]].<ref>{{cite journal |vauthors=Cvitanović S, Zekan L, Capkun V, Marusić M |title=Specific hyposensitization in patients allergic to ''Parietaria officinalis'' pollen allergen |journal=J Investig Allergol Clin Immunol |volume=4 |issue=6 |pages=283–290 |date=1994 |pmid=7735515 }}</ref>

== Uses ==
It was once used in the making of certain [[mead|metheglin]]s.{{fact|date=October 2016}}

==Chemistry==

The leaves and flowers of ''P. officinalis'' contain the [[flavonoids]] kaempferol-3-[[bioside]], the 3-glucosides and 3-rutinosides of [[quercetin]], [[kaempferol]] and [[isorhamnetin]], 3-sophorosides of quercetin and kaempferol and 3-neohesperosides of kaempferol and isorhamnetin.<ref>{{cite journal |title=Flavonoids of ''Parietaria officinalis'' |first1=J. |last1=Budzianowki |first2=L. |last2=Skrzypczak |first3=D. |last3=Walkowiak |journal=J. Nat. Prod. |year=1985 |volume=48 |issue=2 |pages=336–7 |doi=10.1021/np50038a033}}</ref> They also contain [[Caffeoylmalic acid|caffeoylmalic]] and two [[pyrrole]] acids.<ref>{{cite journal |title=Caffeoylmalic and two pyrrole acids from ''Parietaria officinalis'' |first=Jaromir |last=Budzianowski |journal=Phytochemistry |year=1990 |volume=29 |issue=10 |pages=3299–3301  |doi=10.1016/0031-9422(90)80203-S|bibcode=1990PChem..29.3299B }}</ref>

== See also ==
It is in a different family from ''[[Anacyclus pyrethrum]]'', also called pellitory.

